# Black-box delivery scenarios

This Playbook takes a project, a confirmed requirements JSON file, and the installed path of this Skill. Its sole deliverable is a reviewed set of black-box delivery acceptance scenarios. A runnable application, application code, devices, test accounts, credentials, third-party integrations, resource preflight, white-box scenarios, and execution evidence are not prerequisites. Never ask Boss for the location of an unimplemented app in this workflow. Never declare application delivery accepted here.

The ledger is `<project>/.delivery-acceptance/`, is Git-ignored, and follows `<skill>/references/data-model.md`. The deterministic stage checker is `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box`. Never put real credentials in the ledger. This is already an active Playbook session: neither Captain nor any role may invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside it. All role outcomes leave Git HEAD and Git-visible worktree unchanged.

Roles:

- Analyst
- Reviewer

Analyst creates and repairs scenarios and methods under the ignored ledger. Reviewer independently checks requirement coverage and scenario meaning. Captain reports the terminal result. Reviewer must not modify Analyst's ledger; only Analyst repairs it. The deterministic `script` actor independently reruns the stage checker after a positive Reviewer verdict. Only a zero exit may produce `scenariosReady`. A failed checker returns to Analyst for repair, at most three review rounds. No Tester role and no test execution in this workflow.

### ENTRY-1

When the playbook receives the project, the requirements JSON file, and the installed Skill path, Captain shall prompt Analyst:

> Verify that the supplied requirements file contains a confirmed version with source and stable, unique IDs with nonempty text.
> If it is absent or ambiguous, report the exact gap; do not invent or approve requirements.
> Otherwise run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py init <project> --requirements <requirements-file>` on first use, or `import-requirements` when the approved source changed.
> If an initialized ledger already has the same requirements snapshot, reuse it without deleting prior decisions.
> A changed approved requirements snapshot invalidates prior scenario completeness.
> Never put real credentials in the ledger.
> Do not invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside this session.
> > <project>
> > <requirements-file>
> > <skill>

Results:
- `ready`: The requirements file contains a confirmed version with source and stable, unique IDs with nonempty text, and the successful first-use initialization or changed-source import is complete.
- `requirementsGap`: The requirements file is absent or ambiguous; do not invent or approve requirements. Output shall include `failureDetails: <exact requirements gap>`.

### DESIGN-1

When the requirements are ready, Captain shall prompt Analyst:

> Map every confirmed requirement to at least one black-box scenario.
> Cover normal, exception, boundary, and state-change behavior where relevant.
> Keep the scenario observable from the user or external system boundary, and do not claim a particular implementation.
> Record in `.delivery-acceptance/scenarios.json` with `kind: black_box`, `title`, `requirement_ids`, `expected`, and `method_ids`.
> Record reusable AI-operated methods in `.delivery-acceptance/methods.json` with concrete `preconditions`, `actions`, `evidence_expected`, and `description`; these are plans for later execution, not evidence that execution occurred.
> Link existing test-case references only if they are actually present.
> Set `black_box_complete` only after assessing every confirmed ID.
> Do not set `white_box_complete` without reviewing implemented design or code.
> Preserve any existing white-box scenarios and resource decisions; a new requirements import already invalidates completeness.
> Follow the ledger structure in `<skill>/references/data-model.md`; keep the ledger Git-ignored and put no real credentials in it.
> Leave Git HEAD and the Git-visible worktree unchanged.
> Never ask Boss for the location of an unimplemented app in this workflow.
> Do not invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside this session.
> > <project>
> > <skill>

### REVIEW-1

When the scenario draft is ready for independent review or has been repaired, and fewer than three review rounds have occurred, Captain shall prompt Reviewer:

> Inspect the requirements snapshot and the scenario and method ledgers.
> Check that every confirmed ID has meaningful coverage, that expected outcomes are observable and testable, and that normal, exceptional, boundary, and state-change cases are present where relevant.
> Identify specific missing or contradictory behavior by requirement and scenario ID.
> Run `python3 <skill>/scripts/playbook.py check <project> --stage black-box` and report its exact requirements and black-box gate results.
> Do not require the resources, white-box, execution, or review gates to pass at this stage.
> Do not ask Boss to approve an ordinary scenario list.
> Do not modify Analyst's ledger; only Analyst repairs it.
> Do not invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside this session.
> > <project>
> > <skill>

Results:
- `passed`: Independent review finds meaningful, observable, and testable coverage for every confirmed ID, and the direct stage checker reports its requirements and black-box gates pass.
- `gaps`: Independent review finds specific missing or contradictory behavior. Output shall include `failureDetails: <exact failure details with requirement and scenario references>`.

### GATE-1

When independent scenario review has passed, Captain shall run:

> python3 '<skill>'/scripts/playbook.py check '<project>' --stage black-box > '<project>'/.delivery-acceptance/black-box-gate.json

Results:
- `passed`: The stage checker exited with status zero and its black-box gate report is written; `scenariosReady` may follow only on this zero exit.
- `failed`: The stage checker exited with a nonzero status; do not report `scenariosReady`, and return to Analyst for repair while review rounds remain.

### REPAIR-1

When Reviewer identifies material gaps or the black-box stage checker exits nonzero, and fewer than three review rounds have occurred, Captain shall prompt Analyst:

> Repair only the named gaps in the ignored ledger files, preserving confirmed requirement IDs and existing valid scenarios.
> Read the black-box gate report at `<project>/.delivery-acceptance/black-box-gate.json` when the stage checker failed.
> Leave Git HEAD and the Git-visible worktree unchanged.
> Do not invoke `run_workflow.py`, `/black-box-scenarios`, or another Playbook from inside this session.
> > <project>
> > <failureDetails>

### COMPLETION-1

When the Reviewer verdict and the black-box stage gate have passed, or the workflow cannot proceed after the allowed repairs or because the requirements are unavailable, Captain shall report the terminal result:

> Report `scenariosReady` with a nonempty `response` naming the scenario ledger path, the number of black-box scenarios, the requirement coverage, and the black-box gate report.
> Completion is read-only: do not edit any file, including tracked documentation or ledger files, or run more design or checker work.
> Explicitly state that no App behavior was executed or accepted.
> A `scenariosReady` result is a successful design deliverable, not full application acceptance.
> Otherwise report `incomplete` with the exact failure details.
> Never declare application delivery accepted here.
> > <project>
> > <failureDetails>

Results:
- `scenariosReady`: The Reviewer verdict and the black-box stage gate both pass. Output shall include `response: <nonempty report naming the scenario ledger path, the number of black-box scenarios, requirement coverage, and the black-box gate report>`.
- `incomplete`: A black-box gate fails after the allowed repairs, material gaps remain after three rounds, or the requirements are unavailable. Output shall include `response: <nonempty report of the exact failure details>`.

